WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.560
How many distinct cards paid at one merchant this hour?

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:14.489
A set of card ids answers exactly, but a hundred thousand ids took 4.4 megabytes, and there are 20,000 merchants.

3
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:17.359
A counter is tiny, but it counts payments.

4
00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:23.704
A card that pays three times counts three times, and skipping repeats means remembering every card.

5
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.584
So give up exactness.

6
00:00:25.584 --> 00:00:27.028
Hash each card id.

7
00:00:27.028 --> 00:00:31.920
A good hash looks random: every bit is 0 or 1 with even odds.

8
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:37.396
Count the zeros at its right end, before the first 1, and add one: that's the rank.

9
00:00:37.396 --> 00:00:41.671
Half of all hashes have rank 1, a quarter rank 2, an eighth rank 3.

10
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:45.644
So a rank of 4 turns up about once in 16 distinct cards.

11
00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:50.679
If the highest rank you've seen is 4, you've probably seen about 16 cards.

12
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:56.271
A card that pays again has the same hash and the same rank, so repeats change nothing.

13
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:58.648
But one record is a wild guess.

14
00:00:58.648 --> 00:01:03.221
One lucky card with 20 zeros would claim about two million cards.

15
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.731
So keep many records.

16
00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:10.816
The last bits of the hash pick a register, and each register keeps the highest rank it has seen.

17
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:13.981
card B lands in register 5 with rank 1.

18
00:01:13.981 --> 00:01:20.164
card C lands in register 3 with rank 4, and the register keeps the larger value.

19
00:01:20.164 --> 00:01:21.632
It never goes down.

20
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.752
After many cards, one register may be lucky.

21
00:01:24.752 --> 00:01:33.675
Redis takes a harmonic mean of 2 to the power of each register, which damps the lucky ones, and scales it by the number of registers.

22
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:44.697
Redis keeps 16,384 registers of 6 bits each, 12,288 bytes, whether it has seen a thousand cards or a billion.

23
00:01:44.697 --> 00:01:48.164
The standard error is 0.81 percent.

24
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:50.731
A HyperLogLog never stores a card.

25
00:01:50.731 --> 00:01:57.925
It keeps the longest runs of zeros, and counts distinct cards in 12 kilobytes, within about 1 percent.
